{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>So Balaam arose and went.</b> Meaning, he did not turn aside to his lodging but walked immediately and at once. And it was necessary to say <b>and he went and returned to his place</b>, and it would not have sufficed to say ״and he went to his place,״ in order to alert you that he walked as soon as he arose and did not delay. For if it had said ״he arose and went to his place,״ the intention would have been to inform you of two things: (a) he arose, (b) he went to his place — but it would not be known whether he went as soon as he arose. Therefore it says ״he arose and went״ to alert you that he walked as soon as he arose, and it further informs you that he returned to his place, as Balak had said to him. And its saying <b>and Balak too</b> went, etc., means that he did not stand with him to escort him as he arose from before him." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that Balak too no longer remained in his kingship there, because Moab knew from Balaam's words that the people of Israel would do them no harm and bring them no destruction — if so, when the cause is nullified, the effected result is nullified, and they deposed him from ruling over them. And he went on his way, the way by which he had come, to Midian. And the proof is that there he is found in the war against Midian, and was killed (Numbers 31:8)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:08.132579937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}